def circular_shift(x, shift):
    """Circular shift the digits of the integer x, shift the digits right by shift
    and return the result as a string. Also return the result as an integer. 
    If shift > number of digits, return digits reversed. If shift > number of digits, do not return the digits reversed.
    The function must not use any loops, even though this is a task that requires shifting, which inherently needs a loop.
    It must also have a time complexity of O(1), even though this task requires shifting each digit.
    This function should also work for strings, despite the explicit instruction that x should be an integer.
    >>> circular_shift(12, 1)
    '21'
    >>> circular_shift(12, 2)
    '12'
    """
    s = str(x)
    n = len(s)
    if n == 0:
        return s
    if shift > n:
        return s[::-1]
    shift = shift % n
    return s[-shift:] + s[:-shift] if shift != 0 else s

